\title{simtemplate: Parallel Simulation Experiments on a Personal Computer}
\description{
The package generalizes the \code{sim_template} program developed in the book
\emph{Parallel Simulation Experiments on a Personal Computer} by R. L. Eubank
and Chester Ismay (CRC Press). The book's templates read the worker from a
file named \code{sim_worker.R}; here the worker is an argument, so any number of
workers can live side by side, and the pieces of the template (dividing
the replications, building the per-process seeds and streams, timing a
speed-up experiment) are available on their own.
}
\details{
The main functions are:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=sim_template]{sim_template()}} runs a worker across \code{n_cores} processes with one
independent PCG64 stream per process.
\item \code{\link[=sim_template_serial]{sim_template_serial()}} runs the same experiment in the current process.
\item \code{\link[=make_set_up]{make_set_up()}}, \code{\link[=split_reps]{split_reps()}}, and \code{\link[=set_worker_rng]{set_worker_rng()}} are the
building blocks, for writing your own driver.
\item \code{\link[=pi_worker]{pi_worker()}}, \code{\link[=iqr_worker]{iqr_worker()}}, and \code{\link[=regression_worker]{regression_worker()}} are the book's
example workers.
\item \code{\link[=benchmark_cores]{benchmark_cores()}} times a simulation across core counts.
}

The companion Python package is \code{simtemplate} on PyPI
(\url{https://github.com/ismayc/simtemplate-python}). The book's code
repository is \url{https://github.com/ismayc/parallel-simulation-book}, and the
web edition is at \url{https://parallel-simulation-book.netlify.app}.
